<h2>The road, stage by stage</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Petition.</strong> The petition pleads the marriage, the jurisdictional facts, the ground with particularised incidents, and the statutory requirements of Section 23 (no condonation, no collusion, no improper delay). Annexures: marriage proof, photographs, relevant correspondence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Service and appearance.</strong> Summons issue; evasion of service produces substituted service and, ultimately, ex parte proceedings — which remain vulnerable to being set aside on sufficient cause, so petitioners should perfect service rather than race past it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Written statement.</strong> The respondent answers para-wise, pleads any counter-case, and may file a counter-claim for divorce or other matrimonial relief on their own grounds — a step that changes the trial&rsquo;s architecture, since both sides then carry burdens.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Reconciliation and mediation.</strong> The statutory settlement effort: judge-led reconciliation and reference to the court-annexed mediation centre. Everything said there is without prejudice; everything signed there can end the litigation comprehensively.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>5. Interim applications.</strong> Section 24 HMA maintenance and expenses (built on both parties&rsquo; affidavits of assets and income), interim custody and visitation, and any DV Act reliefs claimed in the pending case. These hearings often consume more court time than the main petition.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>6. Issues.</strong> The court frames the questions the trial will answer — the ground, the Section 23 bars, any counter-claim — and the parties file their lists of witnesses and documents.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>7. Petitioner&rsquo;s evidence.</strong> Examination-in-chief by affidavit, exhibition of documents, and cross-examination — the stage where matrimonial cases are usually won or lost, since unchallenged testimony stands and untested allegations fall.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>8. Respondent&rsquo;s evidence.</strong> The mirror stage, with the same disciplines applied to the defence and any counter-claim.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>9. Arguments and judgment.</strong> Final hearing on the evidence and the Section 23 checklist; decree of dissolution, dismissal, or partial relief (including on a counter-claim), with permanent alimony under Section 25 capable of being settled at or after the decree.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>10. Appeal.</strong> A regular appeal lies to the High Court under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act within the prescribed period — and the possibility of appeal is a reason final settlements often arrive at the arguments stage.</div>
</div>

<h2>What distinguishes the Family Court forum</h2>
<p>Three features change how the trial feels. Proceedings are comparatively informal — the judge engages parties directly, and the Family Courts Act permits reception of evidence with relaxed formality in aid of settlement and truth-finding. Parties appear personally far more than in ordinary civil litigation; several stages, especially reconciliation, require it. And the court controls publicity: matrimonial proceedings may be held in camera, and cause-title anonymisation protects parties in reported orders. None of this dilutes the burden of proof: the ground pleaded must still be established by evidence that survives cross-examination.</p>

<h2>The decisions that matter most</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Choose the ground honestly.</strong> Plead the ground the evidence can carry. A petition overloaded with every available ground invites cross-examination on the weakest and taints the strongest.</p>
  <p><strong>Treat the asset affidavit as testimony.</strong> It anchors every maintenance determination and follows the deponent through the case; inaccuracy is the most expensive drafting error in matrimonial practice.</p>
  <p><strong>Use mediation strategically, not ritually.</strong> Arrive with a worked-out structure — residence, monthly support, custody schedule, withdrawal of cross-cases — because comprehensive settlements succeed where piecemeal ones relapse.</p>
  <p><strong>Keep parallel cases coordinated.</strong> Maintenance under Section 144 BNSS, DV proceedings and custody petitions generate overlapping orders; disclose each in every forum and reconcile reliefs, or credibility pays the price.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer maps the general course of a contested divorce and is not advice on any matter. Sequence and pace vary with the court, the personal law applicable, and the parties&rsquo; own procedural choices.</div>
